-**Parameters:** `heightFactor` (float, typical value 1) and `Excavate` (bool) [S1, unofficial].
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:** Boundary pixels lack neighbors on one or more sides; the implementation checks bounds explicitly: `if (param.X - 1 >= 0 && param.X + 1 < param.GrayscaleImage.Width && param.Y - 1 >= 0 && param.Y + 1 < param.GrayscaleImage.Height)` [S1, unofficial]. If out of bounds, it defaults to adding voxels from 0 to the computed height [S1, unofficial]. When excavating, if the computed height is less than or equal to the neighbor minimum, the algorithm places a single voxel at `max(0, height - 1)` instead of an empty column or negative height [S1, unofficial].
+**Parameters:**
+- `heightFactor`: Controls the vertical scale. Default 1 [S1, unofficial].
+- `Excavate`: Boolean. Enables clipping to neighbor minimums [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Boundary Pixels:** Pixels on the edge lack neighbors on one or more sides. The implementation checks bounds explicitly (`if (param.X - 1 >= 0 && param.X + 1 < param.GrayscaleImage.Width && param.Y - 1 >= 0 && param.Y + 1 < param.GrayscaleImage.Height)`) [S1, unofficial]. If out of bounds, it defaults to adding voxels from 0 to the computed height [S1, unofficial].
+- **Negative/Zero Height:** When excavating, if the computed height is less than or equal to the neighbor minimum, the algorithm places a single voxel at `max(0, height - 1)` instead of an empty column or negative height [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision:** The formula uses the constant 65535 for normalization. Implementers must verify the input image's bit-depth matches this scaling factor, as 8-bit images (0-255) will yield near-zero heights unless `heightFactor` is adjusted or the denominator is modified [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$, where $W$ and $H$ are image dimensions [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H \times H_{max})$ for the output voxel list [G].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; generates terrain-like structures efficiently; "Excavate" mode reduces vertical artifacts [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Resolution is limited by the input image resolution; height resolution is limited by `heightFactor` and pixel precision [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** Converting 2D heightmaps or grayscale images into 3D voxel data for games or simulations [G].
-- **When not to use:** When high-resolution surface geometry is required [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$, where $W$ and $H$ are image dimensions. Neighbor lookups are constant-time (4 neighbors), preserving linear scaling [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(W \times H)$ for the output voxel list. Maximum voxel count scales with $W \times H \times H_{max}$ [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; generates terrain-like structures efficiently; "Excavate" mode reduces vertical artifacts and creates smoother transitions [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Resolution is strictly bound to the input image dimensions; height resolution depends on `heightFactor` and channel precision; boundary pixels may exhibit uneven edges due to missing neighbor data [S1, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** Converting 2D heightmaps or grayscale images into 3D voxel data for games or simulations where performance is critical and high-fidelity surface detail is not required [S1, unofficial].
+- **When not to use:** When high-resolution surface geometry is needed (use mesh extraction instead); when the input image is not a heightmap (e.g., color textures should be mapped differently) [S1, unofficial].
 
